1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:03,053
A baby's gold ring in the drawer.

2
00:00:03,053 --> 00:00:06,070
Sell it at a jeweler today and you get 635,000 won.

3
00:00:06,870 --> 00:00:10,597
International gold is down about 30% from its January peak.

4
00:00:10,597 --> 00:00:11,860
Should you sell now?

5
00:00:12,660 --> 00:00:14,953
The short answer: one number has been a better

6
00:00:14,953 --> 00:00:16,398
guide than any gold forecast.

7
00:00:16,398 --> 00:00:19,090
The relationship between interest rates and inflation.

8
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:21,406
History is the evidence.

9
00:00:21,406 --> 00:00:24,607
In the 2008 crisis gold also fell almost 30% first,

10
00:00:24,607 --> 00:00:28,560
yet even buyers at the top broke even within a year and a half.

11
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:32,161
But anyone who bought gold at the 1980 peak

12
00:00:32,161 --> 00:00:34,440
needed 28 years just to break even.

13
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:36,615
And in between came 1998,

14
00:00:36,615 --> 00:00:39,200
when 3.5 million Koreans lined up at banks with

15
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:40,740
the gold from their drawers.

16
00:00:41,550 --> 00:00:43,326
Same gold: once a year and a half,

17
00:00:43,326 --> 00:00:45,414
once 28 years. What made the difference?

18
00:00:45,414 --> 00:00:46,302
In eight minutes,

19
00:00:46,302 --> 00:00:49,540
three stories of gold's great falls will show you that number.

20
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:51,088
First, now.

21
00:00:51,088 --> 00:00:54,282
After nine months sliding from the January top,

22
00:00:54,282 --> 00:00:56,932
international gold fell another 6.8% in

23
00:00:56,932 --> 00:00:59,990
October: its worst October in eighteen years.

24
00:01:00,790 --> 00:01:02,560
The odd part was the timing.

25
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:06,162
On the day US chip and AI stocks plunged in late October,

26
00:01:06,162 --> 00:01:08,690
gold, the safe asset, fell the same day.

27
00:01:09,490 --> 00:01:11,493
Cash is the reason most cited.

28
00:01:11,493 --> 00:01:15,166
Investors who borrowed to buy stocks needed money fast,

29
00:01:15,166 --> 00:01:19,106
so they sold the thing that turns into cash quickest: gold.

30
00:01:19,106 --> 00:01:20,776
Not because gold was bad,

31
00:01:20,776 --> 00:01:23,380
but because it was the easiest to sell.

32
00:01:24,170 --> 00:01:24,906
On top of that,

33
00:01:24,906 --> 00:01:27,410
the Iran war pushed oil above 110 dollars a barrel,

34
00:01:27,410 --> 00:01:28,735
and the US Federal Reserve,

35
00:01:28,735 --> 00:01:29,914
worried about inflation,

36
00:01:29,914 --> 00:01:31,190
raised rates in September.

37
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:33,929
Rate hikes are bad news for gold,

38
00:01:33,929 --> 00:01:35,273
which pays no interest.

39
00:01:35,273 --> 00:01:37,903
And we have seen this before: almost the same

40
00:01:37,903 --> 00:01:39,890
thing happened eighteen years ago.

41
00:01:40,680 --> 00:01:42,414
March 2008: Bear Stearns,

42
00:01:42,414 --> 00:01:45,188
America's fifth-largest investment bank,

43
00:01:45,188 --> 00:01:46,436
came to the brink.

44
00:01:46,436 --> 00:01:49,696
In that fear gold topped 1,000 dollars an ounce

45
00:01:49,696 --> 00:01:51,360
for the first time ever.

46
00:01:52,170 --> 00:01:53,523
Then on September 15,

47
00:01:53,523 --> 00:01:56,358
Lehman Brothers went bankrupt. 'In a crisis,

48
00:01:56,358 --> 00:01:59,580
buy gold,' everyone thought it would rise further.

49
00:02:00,380 --> 00:02:02,207
Gold went the other way.

50
00:02:02,207 --> 00:02:04,796
From above 1,000 dollars in March,

51
00:02:04,796 --> 00:02:08,450
it slid to the 700s by October, almost 30% down.

52
00:02:09,250 --> 00:02:11,257
The reason was the same as now.

53
00:02:11,257 --> 00:02:14,106
Funds and investors hit by stock losses sold

54
00:02:14,106 --> 00:02:16,437
everything they could to raise cash,

55
00:02:16,437 --> 00:02:18,120
and gold was no exception.

56
00:02:18,920 --> 00:02:22,220
The bottom came in late October; in December

57
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:25,070
the Fed cut rates to practically zero,

58
00:02:25,070 --> 00:02:27,770
and money-printing QE soon followed.

59
00:02:28,570 --> 00:02:30,928
Here is the number from the start.

60
00:02:30,928 --> 00:02:34,880
Deposit interest minus inflation: the real interest rate.

61
00:02:35,080 --> 00:02:37,226
If interest is zero while prices rise,

62
00:02:37,226 --> 00:02:40,050
money in the bank loses a little value every year.

63
00:02:40,850 --> 00:02:42,978
With interest below inflation, gold,

64
00:02:42,978 --> 00:02:44,338
which pays no interest,

65
00:02:44,338 --> 00:02:46,230
became the asset that lost less.

66
00:02:47,030 --> 00:02:50,343
In September 2011 gold passed 1,900 dollars,

67
00:02:50,343 --> 00:02:54,560
a record: 2.7 times its October 2008 low in three years.

68
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:57,910
So do we just wait this time? First,

69
00:02:57,910 --> 00:03:01,380
the year gold became a whole country's insurance.

70
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:04,641
Winter 1997: Korea's currency crisis.

71
00:03:04,641 --> 00:03:07,768
The country ran out of dollars and the won lost

72
00:03:07,768 --> 00:03:09,630
half its value within weeks.

73
00:03:10,430 --> 00:03:13,110
In January 1998 broadcasters and banks launched

74
00:03:13,110 --> 00:03:14,650
a gold-collecting campaign.

75
00:03:14,650 --> 00:03:15,619
Over four months,

76
00:03:15,619 --> 00:03:18,870
about 3.5 million people queued at banks with their gold.

77
00:03:18,870 --> 00:03:22,292
Most of it was not donated: banks bought it at market price,

78
00:03:22,292 --> 00:03:23,090
paying in won.

79
00:03:23,880 --> 00:03:26,175
Baby rings, wedding rings, necklaces.

80
00:03:26,175 --> 00:03:28,903
Cardinal Kim Sou-hwan gave the gold cross he

81
00:03:28,903 --> 00:03:30,950
received when he became cardinal.

82
00:03:31,750 --> 00:03:35,356
Some 227 tonnes: more than twice the 104 tonnes

83
00:03:35,356 --> 00:03:37,658
the Bank of Korea holds today.

84
00:03:37,658 --> 00:03:40,266
About 17 don per person. Exported,

85
00:03:40,266 --> 00:03:45,330
it raised about 2.2 billion dollars for an empty national reserve.

86
00:03:46,130 --> 00:03:47,636
Here is the hidden point.

87
00:03:47,636 --> 00:03:49,202
Gold is priced in dollars,

88
00:03:49,202 --> 00:03:50,768
so when the won collapsed,

89
00:03:50,768 --> 00:03:52,455
gold measured in won jumped.

90
00:03:52,455 --> 00:03:56,370
The sturdiest insurance in every home was the gold in the drawer.

91
00:03:57,170 --> 00:04:00,248
The rings and necklaces on those bank counters

92
00:04:00,248 --> 00:04:03,325
would be worth over 38 trillion won at today's

93
00:04:03,325 --> 00:04:04,730
jeweler buying price.

94
00:04:05,530 --> 00:04:07,914
But gold was not always this reliable.

95
00:04:07,914 --> 00:04:10,800
There was a time when gold buyers regretted it

96
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:12,180
for over twenty years.

97
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:17,172
1979: revolution in Iran, oil prices soar,

98
00:04:17,172 --> 00:04:21,563
US inflation tops 13%. Iran, oil, inflation.

99
00:04:21,563 --> 00:04:23,160
Sounds familiar.

100
00:04:23,950 --> 00:04:27,001
People rushed into gold, and on January 21,

101
00:04:27,001 --> 00:04:29,342
1980 it hit 850 dollars an ounce,

102
00:04:29,342 --> 00:04:32,960
more than twenty times its price ten years earlier.

103
00:04:33,770 --> 00:04:37,746
Then Fed Chairman Paul Volcker took the opposite medicine.

104
00:04:37,746 --> 00:04:39,049
To crush inflation,

105
00:04:39,049 --> 00:04:41,860
he raised the policy rate as high as 20%.

106
00:04:42,650 --> 00:04:43,967
For years afterward,

107
00:04:43,967 --> 00:04:47,062
deposit rates beat inflation by several points:

108
00:04:47,062 --> 00:04:48,840
the exact opposite of 2008.

109
00:04:49,040 --> 00:04:50,902
Money grew just sitting in the bank,

110
00:04:50,902 --> 00:04:52,867
so there was less reason to hold gold,

111
00:04:52,867 --> 00:04:53,850
which pays nothing.

112
00:04:54,650 --> 00:04:58,779
Gold bought at 850 was in the 250s by 1999:

113
00:04:58,779 --> 00:05:01,948
down 70% for nearly twenty years.

114
00:05:01,948 --> 00:05:05,885
It only passed 850 again in January 2008.

115
00:05:05,885 --> 00:05:09,150
Buyers at the top waited 28 years.

116
00:05:09,950 --> 00:05:12,030
In the 1990s, unlike today,

117
00:05:12,030 --> 00:05:14,494
central banks were selling gold.

118
00:05:14,494 --> 00:05:16,189
In the summer of 1999,

119
00:05:16,189 --> 00:05:20,502
as Britain began auctioning off more than half its gold,

120
00:05:20,502 --> 00:05:22,120
the price hit bottom.

121
00:05:22,920 --> 00:05:23,987
In dollar terms,

122
00:05:23,987 --> 00:05:27,055
a baby ring given at a first-birthday party in

123
00:05:27,055 --> 00:05:29,590
1980 broke even when that baby was 28.

124
00:05:30,380 --> 00:05:32,483
In won, though, it was different.

125
00:05:32,483 --> 00:05:34,777
When the won collapsed in late 1997,

126
00:05:34,777 --> 00:05:37,453
gold priced in won briefly passed its 1980

127
00:05:37,453 --> 00:05:39,620
peak: the very crisis we just saw.

128
00:05:40,430 --> 00:05:44,150
So is today closer to 2008, or to 1980?

129
00:05:44,940 --> 00:05:48,738
The benchmark for world gold is the US real rate.

130
00:05:48,738 --> 00:05:51,760
Today the US policy rate is 3.75 to 4%.

131
00:05:51,760 --> 00:05:55,402
The Fed's preferred inflation gauge was up 3.6%

132
00:05:55,402 --> 00:05:57,960
from a year earlier in September.

133
00:05:58,760 --> 00:06:02,249
Interest has barely caught up with inflation.

134
00:06:02,249 --> 00:06:04,110
Not far below like 2008,

135
00:06:04,110 --> 00:06:08,530
not far above like the 1980s: the scale has just leveled.

136
00:06:09,330 --> 00:06:11,236
Compare the run-ups too.

137
00:06:11,236 --> 00:06:14,969
Gold rose 24-fold in the ten years before 1980,

138
00:06:14,969 --> 00:06:18,464
about 4-fold in the seven years before 2008,

139
00:06:18,464 --> 00:06:22,753
and more than 3-fold from autumn 2022 to this January.

140
00:06:22,753 --> 00:06:27,360
Which kind of top it was only ever became clear afterward.

141
00:06:28,160 --> 00:06:30,396
That is why next month matters.

142
00:06:30,396 --> 00:06:32,343
On October 28 the Fed held,

143
00:06:32,343 --> 00:06:34,506
but two members voted to hike.

144
00:06:34,506 --> 00:06:39,050
Markets see better than 70% odds of another hike on December 9.

145
00:06:39,860 --> 00:06:42,451
If rates rise and inflation cools,

146
00:06:42,451 --> 00:06:47,557
interest pulls ahead of prices and the scale can tilt against gold,

147
00:06:47,557 --> 00:06:48,700
like the 1980s.

148
00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:52,587
If inflation runs higher and rates fail to keep up,

149
00:06:52,587 --> 00:06:54,416
it can tilt toward 2008.

150
00:06:54,416 --> 00:06:58,760
And what markets already expect may be largely priced in.

151
00:06:59,560 --> 00:07:02,879
For us in Korea there is one more variable: the exchange rate.

152
00:07:02,879 --> 00:07:04,485
Even if global gold stays put,

153
00:07:04,485 --> 00:07:06,519
a weaker won lifts Korean gold prices,

154
00:07:06,519 --> 00:07:08,178
and a stronger won lowers them.

155
00:07:08,178 --> 00:07:09,570
1998 was the extreme case.

156
00:07:10,360 --> 00:07:13,276
Gold has not always followed this scale.

157
00:07:13,276 --> 00:07:15,754
From 2023 US rates beat inflation,

158
00:07:15,754 --> 00:07:17,940
yet gold kept setting records.

159
00:07:17,940 --> 00:07:20,783
The hand that tipped it: central banks,

160
00:07:20,783 --> 00:07:23,917
which bought 170 tonnes through August this

161
00:07:23,917 --> 00:07:26,760
year by the World Gold Council's count.

162
00:07:27,560 --> 00:07:30,436
Since Russia's dollar reserves were frozen in 2022,

163
00:07:30,436 --> 00:07:32,860
many analysts say more countries stack gold

164
00:07:32,860 --> 00:07:34,552
instead of dollars: insurance,

165
00:07:34,552 --> 00:07:36,638
like Korea in 1998. Some buy on dips,

166
00:07:36,638 --> 00:07:38,104
which has cushioned falls,

167
00:07:38,104 --> 00:07:39,570
but there is no guarantee.

168
00:07:40,370 --> 00:07:42,301
Back to the ring in the drawer.

169
00:07:42,301 --> 00:07:45,540
Buying one don at a jeweler today costs 742,000 won.

170
00:07:45,540 --> 00:07:47,097
Selling gets you 635,000.

171
00:07:47,097 --> 00:07:50,460
The buying price includes 10% VAT and a dealer margin.

172
00:07:50,460 --> 00:07:51,706
If you plan to sell,

173
00:07:51,706 --> 00:07:54,820
compare buying prices at two or three shops first.

174
00:07:55,620 --> 00:07:57,742
Sell now and buy back later,

175
00:07:57,742 --> 00:08:01,000
and gold must fall more than 14% before the

176
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,273
same money buys one don again.

177
00:08:03,273 --> 00:08:06,683
So the question is less about predicting gold

178
00:08:06,683 --> 00:08:09,790
than about when you will need this money.

179
00:08:10,600 --> 00:08:12,216
So watch three things. One,

180
00:08:12,216 --> 00:08:14,370
the Fed decision on December 9. Two,

181
00:08:14,370 --> 00:08:15,627
monthly US inflation.

182
00:08:15,627 --> 00:08:18,379
The gap between them is today's number. Three,

183
00:08:18,379 --> 00:08:21,970
the World Gold Council's monthly central bank purchase data.

184
00:08:22,760 --> 00:08:25,644
Over the long run gold went from 850 dollars in

185
00:08:25,644 --> 00:08:27,301
1980 to around 3,900 today.

186
00:08:27,301 --> 00:08:28,712
Adjusted for inflation,

187
00:08:28,712 --> 00:08:31,535
Bloomberg says gold only beat its 1980 peak in

188
00:08:31,535 --> 00:08:33,376
September last year: 45 years,

189
00:08:33,376 --> 00:08:36,996
old enough for that baby to buy a ring for their own child.

190
00:08:36,996 --> 00:08:38,715
The road was never straight,

191
00:08:38,715 --> 00:08:41,599
and its slope was mostly set by the gap between

192
00:08:41,599 --> 00:08:43,010
interest and inflation.

193
00:08:43,820 --> 00:08:46,134
In a crisis gold was sold first,

194
00:08:46,134 --> 00:08:49,677
and when interest lagged inflation it rose first.

195
00:08:49,677 --> 00:08:51,557
Next time the Fed decides,

196
00:08:51,557 --> 00:08:53,509
set it beside US inflation,

197
00:08:53,509 --> 00:08:55,606
then add the won-dollar rate,

198
00:08:55,606 --> 00:08:58,860
and you can read why that ring's value moves.

199
00:08:59,650 --> 00:09:01,878
When did your family's baby ring arrive?

200
00:09:01,878 --> 00:09:03,215
Tell us in the comments,

201
00:09:03,215 --> 00:09:05,610
and subscribe for more stories about money.

202
00:09:05,810 --> 00:09:09,141
Why gold swung so much this year is in our previous gold video.

203
00:09:09,141 --> 00:09:09,670
Thank you.
